[1mThe hard part was [38;5;10mfoo_t[0m's layout[22m, since [38;5;10mprint_foo[0m takes a pointer to it and callers can construct one. I confirmed empirically rather than assumed: gcc on x86-64 packs [38;5;10munsigned int x:2[0m, [38;5;10munsigned int y:3[0m, and [38;5;10mbool b:1[0m into a single byte (bits 0–1, 2–4, 5) despite [38;5;10mb[0m having a different declared type, then places [38;5;10mint z[0m at offset 4, for [38;5;10msizeof == 8[0m, [38;5;10malignof == 4[0m. The Rust struct is [38;5;10m[u8; 4][0m + [38;5;10mc_int[0m so bit positions don't depend on endianness, and [38;5;10mconst[0m assertions now enforce size/align/offsets at compile time.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two details from the disassembly worth noting:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mdriver[0m masks the incoming [38;5;10m_Bool[0m byte with [38;5;10mand $0x1[0m before storing it. I declared that parameter as [38;5;10mu8[0m instead of Rust [38;5;10mbool[0m: identical C ABI (one byte in the low bits of the third argument register), but it reproduces the masking for byte values other than 0/1 without incurring Rust UB.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mdriver[0m read-modify-writes an uninitialized stack byte, so [38;5;10mfoo[0m's padding bits are indeterminate in the C. [38;5;10mprint_foo[0m masks every field it reads, so this is unobservable; I zero the padding.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
